## **Introduction**

The ADC peripheral in STM32 microcontrollers converts analog signals to digital values. It supports multiple channels, including internal channels such as VREFINT (internal reference voltage), temperature sensor, and battery voltage.

Using multiple channels in scan mode allows sequential sampling of several inputs. This tutorial shows how to configure and use two channels: one internal (VREFINT) and one external, to read analog values efficiently with STM32CubeMX2 and STM32Cube for Visual Studio Code.

## Prerequisites

Ensure that you have installed:

* [STM32CubeMX2](https://www.st.com/content/st_com/en/stm32cubemx.html?tab=installer#stm32-software-downloads-container)

* The latest STM32C5 HAL2 driver

* [STM32CubeIDE extension](https://www.st.com/en/development-tools/stm32vscode.html) for Visual Studio Code

The hardware used in this tutorial is the [NUCLEO-C562RE](https://www.st.com/en/evaluation-tools/nucleo-c562re.html) board.

## 1. STM32CubeMX2 project creation and basic setup

Follow these steps to create an application project for the NUCLEO-C562RE board. This exercise creates a simple USART application.

### 1.1 Project creation

Open STM32CubeMX2. On the Home page, click the**[MCU square]** to create a new project.

![BMontanari_0-1770300899565.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/a22ea319-9cb3-4cc1-c13d-de6d34490ccc.png "BMontanari_0-1770300899565.png")

In the search field under **MCU name**, enter **STM32C562RE** and select your board. Click **[****Continue]**.

![BMontanari_1-1770300899568.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/7f1c24e8-3ca7-8c24-75a2-5fed3c84befe.png "BMontanari_1-1770300899568.png")

Enter your project name and location. Click **[Automatically Download, Install & Create Project]** to finish project creation.

![BMontanari_2-1770300899573.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/46361926-3025-d2b7-e57e-5e6c38245910.png "BMontanari_2-1770300899573.png")

Select **[Launch Project]** to start.

![BMontanari_3-1770300899576.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/6145146f-732e-6451-01a3-ee378986d565.png "BMontanari_3-1770300899576.png")

### 1.2 Pinout and configuration

The ADC can be configured in several different modes, but for simplicity, two channels are used to showcase the typical application. A continuous conversion, scanning the channels.

Go to **Peripherals → Analog → ADC1** and enable **ADC1**.

![BMontanari_4-1770300899580.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/0574f644-d7b9-0336-3896-5144abe948d6.png "BMontanari_4-1770300899580.png")

Enable Channel 1 and Channel 2, those will be allocated at PA0 and PA1, readily available at CN7 and CN8.

![BMontanari_5-1770300899585.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/53f88387-e4d8-6ebb-398a-9db1164a4e2c.png "BMontanari_5-1770300899585.png")

Configure the channels to have the desired sampling rate and to work as single-ended.

![BMontanari_6-1770300899587.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/542cf137-b484-594e-1a6b-c23046af01dc.png "BMontanari_6-1770300899587.png")

In **ADC group configuration**, select the **Continuous Mode**, sequencer with 2 ranks, so we can assign each channel an order and then, set the index order.

![BMontanari_7-1770300899593.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/4aa30e5f-58b0-7026-d906-3f62a9803ff7.png "BMontanari_7-1770300899593.png")

Optionally, in this same area, one can enable the NVIC as well as the DMA, but for now, we focus on the polling method.

### 1.3 Optional configuration for printf

In the "Pinout" tab, it’s possible to double check the two pins allocated for the ADC as well as configure the PA2 and PA3 for UART2, to allow printf.

![BMontanari_8-1770300899597.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/4bffea2c-38bb-5257-6a4e-eaed14306f6d.png "BMontanari_8-1770300899597.png")

Configure the UART2.

![BMontanari_9-1770300899604.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/9b8552a3-b9d9-d416-bcad-c0772ace57d7.png "BMontanari_9-1770300899604.png")

![BMontanari_10-1770300899608.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/93c05025-d2d8-768e-3bc2-a2f529d865f3.png "BMontanari_10-1770300899608.png")

### 1.4 Code generation

To generate the code:

1. Click on the **[Project settings]** icon and select the desired IDE.

2. Click the **[Generate]** button.

![BMontanari_11-1770300899613.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/8c22f8ed-5ad3-f337-1b45-b96d08d2e80e.png "BMontanari_11-1770300899613.png")

## 2. Configuring the project in Visual Studio Code

Open Visual Studio Code and open the project folder.

If prompted, select the configuration. If not prompted, press Ctrl+Shift+P, type **CMake: Select Configure Preset**, and choose the debug configuration.

![BMontanari_12-1770300899614.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/ecb64aa2-f251-7482-684b-d1f1ec159dfb.png "BMontanari_12-1770300899614.png")

Build the project to ensure everything is set, then proceed to code implementation.

![BMontanari_14-1770300899617.png](https://uploads-eu-west-1.insided.com/stmicroelectronics-en/attachment/9fc5577a-2933-027e-1ea0-c8d68252a6d7.png "BMontanari_14-1770300899617.png")

## 3. Code editing

### 3.1 ADC Polling mode conversion

This example reads two ADC channels sequentially using polling.

**In main.c:**

```

/**

******************************************************************************

* file : main.c

* brief : Main program body

* Calls target system initialization then loop in main.

******************************************************************************

*

